@MikePFrank @robbensinger @davidad @Learner69420 @KerryLVaughan @bayeslord @BasedBeff One argument (not claiming it's strong, but &gt;0):
- increasing intelligence allows conversion of high-level concepts to  low-level ontologies (e.g. "happiness" -&gt;"these brain states")
- AGIs will reformulate their terminal goals in terms of simple structures, then maximize those

@acidshill Technically that's different from a skill gap but in practice I think that ingraining habits is a big part of the skill.
Also aspie and NT people mean different things by "understanding people" - prioritising explicit words vs underlying state.
https://t.co/SHdw0DURVr

@reconfigurthing A) Slave morality
B) WEIRDness being downstream of Christian disruption of family loyalty norms, as Henrich argues
C) Offering kisses to people who disagree with you is a very Jesus move

@davidmanheim @KellerScholl @michael_nielsen Easy to overestimate movements you're not part of, and underestimate those you are.

The 10s/100s of thousands of people thing is very likely wrong, but also no. of people worldwide is less relevant for tracking EA's influence than no. in elite circles.

https://t.co/4qOApyNgD4

@provisionalidea @VictorLevoso @GiadaPistilli But I do find the argument "working on existing systems will help address future risks" the most persuasive of the three.

My main concern is that it won't help prevent dangerous behavior that emerges at larger scales (as Steinhardt discusses).
https://t.co/NeJKwL3Xci

@GiadaPistilli I respect your decision to focus on the problems you care about most.
But I wish people with your view would explain the details of their disagreements more often. I'd love to hear compelling arguments for not worrying about AGI, but I haven't found any.
https://t.co/nrXMITYFSA

